WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.600
Coming off of the skyscraper highs of episode 3 I&nbsp;
think some people may find episode 4 underwhelming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:11.820
in comparison, but it’s still a great overall&nbsp;
package. It may be slow at parts, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:16.980
that’s necessary in creating a realistic pace.&nbsp;
The scenes in between this time all felt like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.240
they had a purpose and I wouldn’t classify any&nbsp;
of this as filler like I’ve seen people say.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:25.980
A lot of this material relates to Ellie trying&nbsp;
to chip away at Joel’s viewpoint of Ellie being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.900
cargo. Hearing him say that was very crushing and&nbsp;
it seems like Ellie wants a better relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.300
because she first asked Joel if she was family to&nbsp;
him. She’s been trying really hard to bond with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:41.820
him and this resistance he gives off makes their&nbsp;
dialogue scenes really good. It offers a point of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:45.780
conflict within their relationship of whether&nbsp;
or not Joel will open up more in each moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:51.540
and if Ellie will become Sarah in Joel’s eyes. In&nbsp;
each episode so far there has been a progression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:56.820
Like in the last episode, Joel and Ellie were&nbsp;
barely on speaking terms, and now that they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:01.860
Ellie is trying to further push that line to&nbsp;
Joel liking her company. Even for this episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:06.960
their relationship starting point is vastly&nbsp;
different from where it ends. That’s why I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.580
some of these scenes like the car ride dialogue&nbsp;
and the campsite setting were so important because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:16.320
it sets up a point in their relationship that&nbsp;
they then progress from later on. And again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.120
these are really entertaining moments like with&nbsp;
Ellie reading jokes to Joel and trying to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.880
him laugh, and her discovering a porn magazine.&nbsp;
It’s a very human and awkward moment and Ellie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:36.060
using these avenues is a clever way to bond with&nbsp;
Joel. Her attempts at humor are endearing and is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:41.340
a direct contrast to the lifeless nature of the&nbsp;
world and even Joel. She’s essentially slowly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.500
re-invigorating him and seeing this process no&nbsp;
matter how slow it may be will always be great to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:51.840
watch. Like I love this moment where she delivers&nbsp;
her first joke and Pedro Pascal has an extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:57.300
subtle expression as to him kind of liking it,&nbsp;
but suppressing it. You can see his tense face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:03.600
partially ease up and I think this was a good&nbsp;
first step visually for Joel to relax a bit. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.220
recurring beat for their characters I think acts&nbsp;
as a good checkpoint for us to directly compare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:13.140
their growth throughout the episode. Because we go&nbsp;
from Joel tolerating her jokes to finally giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:19.500
in and delivering off a warm laugh. So the plot in&nbsp;
this episode may not pop off, but I still love the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:24.060
dialogue and the type of relationship that Joel&nbsp;
and Ellie have in the show. I also find it kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:29.340
of weird how people are saying this episode isn’t&nbsp;
very good, but yet this features the most of Joel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:34.260
and Ellie together. And I thought that’s what&nbsp;
people wanted so much. Because from a game to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:40.200
show adaption for this episode, it’s almost 1 to&nbsp;
1 besides the inclusion of Kathleen. I think her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:45.060
character felt a bit off because how is this&nbsp;
seemingly non-powerful character in charge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.800
this revolutionary group when she’s surrounded by&nbsp;
these other characters who look visually stronger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.540
Her brief scenes when talking to everyone didn’t&nbsp;
exude a lot of confidence and she didn’t give off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:59.460
strong leader vibes. Also, her introductory&nbsp;
scene didn’t land as hard as it felt like it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:04.500
was intended because of how much missing context&nbsp;
there was surrounding these two characters. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.520
writing here tells you enough to understand&nbsp;
what’s going on, but I don’t think we share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:13.260
a similar emotional connection to what was going&nbsp;
on. Then when she comes back in mid-way through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:19.260
her speech to execute the doctor and then goes&nbsp;
back outside again felt weird pacing-wise. To me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:23.820
this wasn’t the most compelling introduction for&nbsp;
a new villain character and I think it could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:28.980
had a slightly better execution. That and the&nbsp;
nature of her character I feel like would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:34.200
a hard time convincing all of these people with&nbsp;
her lies and manipulation. It makes me want this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:39.120
guy who actually is the voice actor for Tommy&nbsp;
in the game to be the villain. His design is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:44.640
lot more unique and badass and doesn’t resemble a&nbsp;
karen who probably used to be a soccer mom. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.140
overall group though I think is an interesting&nbsp;
exploration as to if a people-run government is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.120
better than the one prior. It illustrates how&nbsp;
selfishly human-driven this group is through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:59.880
Kathleen’s character and is mostly her using&nbsp;
people to accomplish her own goals. It creates a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:04.800
much more personally driven and fleshed-out threat&nbsp;
that surrounds Joel and Ellie in this setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:10.200
because in the game these were basically unnamed&nbsp;
people who wanted loot. Which we can go from this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:15.780
and talk about the ambush because this was a&nbsp;
horrific scene. This was a perfect re-creation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:21.360
from the game and every detail was replicated&nbsp;
with immense precision. Starting out I love the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:26.340
quick reactions that Bella Ramsey gives off where&nbsp;
she’s shocked and dismayed about how Joel is about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:32.100
to run over this guy asking for help. Then them&nbsp;
crashing into the laundromat was a crazy stunt and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:37.140
it looked very realistic. Like there was obviously&nbsp;
no way for them to cheat this since they literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.120
crashed a car into this building and you can see&nbsp;
the stunt actors in the car taking on that crash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:47.700
Easy 10 out of 10 stunt. The shootout following&nbsp;
this was equally good and the aspect about this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:52.920
scene in particular that I loved was the sound&nbsp;
design and music. The shots were so loud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:58.920
you could hear the base of each shot, the bullets&nbsp;
penetrating every single distinct surface making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:03.600
each shot sound unique and realistic, the raiders&nbsp;
mocking and yelling at our characters like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:09.540
NPC’s from the game, and the music slowly building&nbsp;
the entire time making the eventual confrontation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:19.860
of violence that much better. Then the music eases&nbsp;
off as Joel kills one of the raiders in a release&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:25.680
of tension. Just a perfect 1 minute of television&nbsp;
that doesn’t stop here because we now transition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:31.020
into a more suspenseful moment as the raider&nbsp;
enters the building. Seeing this moment from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:36.240
perspective of Ellie enhances this because it’s&nbsp;
more tense not knowing who has the advantage or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:41.040
who is about to die in the room behind her. Even&nbsp;
when Joel shoots the intruder we still shortly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:46.560
question the fact if Joel was the one who shot or&nbsp;
got shot. Then the scene keeps going when we think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:51.780
everything is fine to then having another raider&nbsp;
surprise us from the back door. From a writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:56.880
perspective, this entire scene is so genius&nbsp;
because it breaks up the action into three varied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:02.100
parts. We have the initial explosion of action&nbsp;
that is the car crash and the shootout, the more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:06.360
suspense-driven moment of hiding from the raider,&nbsp;
and the twist of thinking we are in the clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:11.640
to then throwing another dire obstacle at our&nbsp;
protagonists. It creates a good and balanced flow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:17.520
throughout the action scene. Then the last moment&nbsp;
allows Ellie to finally save Joel and she shoots a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:23.460
human being, which on its own is another important&nbsp;
ordeal to explore. This is a masterful scene from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:29.280
writing to execution and the screams and pleading&nbsp;
coming from this poor guy is insanely haunting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.680
Like honestly one of the best performances from&nbsp;
an extra that I’ve seen in a while. Him crying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:39.480
out for his mom really blurred the lines as to if&nbsp;
Joel and Ellie were the bad guys in this scenario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:44.580
because despite them defending themselves, they&nbsp;
are still executing this kid who is no longer a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:49.860
threat. But sadly it’s necessary for them since&nbsp;
he can offer the enemy intel. Having the death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:54.420
of this guy play out through the perspective of&nbsp;
Ellie is a double positive. We can see her rich&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:59.160
performance as she deals with this traumatic&nbsp;
experience and by not directly focusing on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:04.620
death visually it doesn’t make the violence feel&nbsp;
gratuitous. The kind of impact this has on Ellie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:10.080
is very nuanced because emotionally it’s affecting&nbsp;
her, but her practicality overrides that because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.880
she knows that they are still in danger and she&nbsp;
needs to function like an adult. This kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:20.640
perseverance makes me love Ellie because she’s a&nbsp;
strong character, despite being a child. She’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:25.560
immediately becoming incapacitated to emotions&nbsp;
and her instinct of survival is still operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:31.680
at full capacity. An overall great acting moment&nbsp;
for Bella Ramsey. This whole plot point allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:36.600
Joel to finally make an attempt to act like a&nbsp;
father. Ellie has done an unthinkable task of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:41.820
shooting someone as a kid, and Joel can’t help but&nbsp;
feel guilty for having put her in that situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:46.920
regardless of whether or not it was forced upon&nbsp;
them. This to me is what I would identify as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.920 --> 00:07:52.260
the turning point for their relationship together.&nbsp;
Through this traumatic experience, they are deeply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:57.900
and inherently entangled now and it is inescapable&nbsp;
to not deal with the immense emotional baggage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:02.880
executing someone. Joel was trying his hardest&nbsp;
to keep his relationship with Ellie at a surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:07.680
level, but he would be doing a disservice to&nbsp;
himself as a father if he didn’t try to console&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:12.780
Ellie. And since this trait has been unlocked&nbsp;
within Joel we now see him give in to Ellie’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:17.940
humor and before he breaks in a fit of laughter at&nbsp;
the end he makes his own joke towards Ellie here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:31.020
This natural progression between Joel and Ellie&nbsp;
is painstakingly detailed and every interaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:36.240
that they have feels important. That’s why I would&nbsp;
never classify their earlier scenes together as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:41.700
filler because it’s necessary build-up to make&nbsp;
this eventual turn even more powerful. Because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:46.920
the second Joel lets loose and finally laughs, is&nbsp;
probably going to be one of my favorite moments in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:52.800
the show. Such a wholesome moment with excellent&nbsp;
long awaited pay-off. Then for the last aspect I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:57.180
loved was the detail of Joel turning over in his&nbsp;
sleep to where his bad ear was the one exposed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:01.800
Which explains why Henry was able to sneak up&nbsp;
on them. A great writing moment that perfectly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:06.180
explains itself if you paid attention. For&nbsp;
this episode, I have seen a lot of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:10.200
say that it should have been combined with&nbsp;
the next one because it feels incomplete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:15.780
but I think Joel and Ellie’s personal journey&nbsp;
offers a good endpoint for episode 4. Plot-wise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.780 --> 00:09:20.580
it does end on a cliffhanger, but we don’t know&nbsp;
how long the next episode will be. For all we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:25.560
episode 5 could be over an hour long and if they&nbsp;
combined these two episodes it could have been 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:30.720
hours long which would be getting ridiculous.&nbsp;
So a slightly under 50-minute episode I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:36.060
is perfectly acceptable and it does enough within&nbsp;
its time to be satisfying. It may not go as hard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:41.400
as the first 3 episodes, but that’s really tough&nbsp;
competition in of itself from how amazing those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:46.860
ones were. You can’t really have 10 out of 10&nbsp;
episodes constantly and having an 8 every now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.860 --> 00:09:52.740
and then is natural. Especially since this episode&nbsp;
gives our characters a bit of a breather and a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:57.420
chance to develop. Thank you for watching and&nbsp;
by the way, the next episode airs on Friday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.420 --> 00:10:03.360
since HBO doesn’t want to air episode 5 against&nbsp;
the Superbowl. So Imagine being cowards HBO.

